---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { ARM_TESTER, DISTRIBUTIONS, INSTALL_COMMAND, RELEASE, REPO, type Level } from '../data/site';

const levels: Record<Level, string> = {
  daily: 'Main setup',
  vm: 'Tested',
  built: 'Builds only',
};
---

<Page
  title="Ngó Sen: a Vietnamese input method for Linux"
  description="Ngó Sen is a Vietnamese input method for fcitx5 on Linux. One-line install, and an honest list of where it has been tested."
  heading="Ngó Sen"
  lang="en"
>
  <p slot="lede">
    A Vietnamese input method for fcitx5 on Linux. Gõ Sen puts text straight into the app without
    underlining it; address-bar and search suggestions update with each key.
  </p>

  <p>
    Ngó Sen means “lotus stem”. Bugs can be reported in
    <a href={`${REPO}/issues`}>Issues</a>. Other pages are in Vietnamese, except for the bilingual
    page linked below.
  </p>
  <p><a href="/en/typing-without-preedit/">How Vietnamese people type, and how to help.</a></p>
  <p>
    Use Gõ Sen as the main typing mode: open the Ngó Sen settings window, go to Gõ chữ and select
    Gõ Sen in the Chế độ gõ row. Browsers, terminals, Zalo and LibreOffice all use this mode;
    Ngó Sen detects how each app accepts text, so there is no need to switch modes. All packages
    use the Rust composition core except Debian 12, which keeps the Go core.
  </p>

  <h2 id="release">Version {RELEASE.version}</h2>
  <p>
    Version 1.5.5-1 fixes diacritic typing in the Firefox Flatpak address bar during autofill and
    macros with uppercase abbreviations.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Typing diacritics in the address bar of Firefox installed from Flatpak during URL autofill
      no longer garbles words: <code>ee</code> gives <code>ê</code> instead of <code>eê</code>,
      <code>es</code> gives <code>é</code> instead of <code>eé</code>, and <code>dduowcj</code> gives
      <code>được</code> instead of <code>dđược</code>
      (<a href={`${REPO}/issues/200`}>#200</a>, <a href={`${REPO}/pull/204`}>#204</a>).
      Ngó Sen recognises Flatpak apps by their usual names; the typing log records both names,
      such as <code>firefox (org.mozilla.firefox)</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Macros saved with uppercase abbreviations, such as <code>VN</code> → <code>Việt Nam</code>,
      now expand; previously they never matched. If the same abbreviation is saved with different
      capitalisation, Ngó Sen uses the lowercase entry, or the first saved entry if there is none.
      The macro editor treats <code>VN</code> and <code>vn</code> as the same abbreviation and offers
      to update it instead of adding a second entry (<a href={`${REPO}/pull/194`}>#194</a>).
    </li>
    <li>
      In the Firefox address bar during autofill, typing a letter that matches the next letter of
      the suggested address no longer loses the word being typed. Ngó Sen previously mistook it for
      a mouse click: <code>gox</code> stayed <code>gox</code>, and <code>daaus</code> gave
      <code>daáu</code>. They now give <code>gõ</code> and <code>dấu</code>. Tested with Firefox
      installed from Flatpak on Ubuntu GNOME (<a href={`${REPO}/pull/209`}>#209</a>).
    </li>
    <li>
      Added checks to prevent errors when Gõ Sen reopens macros ending in quotation marks or an
      ellipsis, or words using VNI Windows. Automated tests found incorrect reconstruction after
      backspacing into the previous word: the macro <code>q</code> → <code>“quá”</code>, followed
      by Backspace and <code>s</code>, gave <code>“qua”s</code>, or a VNI Windows word gave
      <code>ñöä</code>. This has not been encountered in apps: the previous version did not show
      the error in tests on Ubuntu GNOME and Linux Mint in GTK text fields, LibreOffice Writer
      and xterm. The change is preventive; ordinary words can still be reopened in VNI Windows /
      TCVN3 (<a href={`${REPO}/pull/199`}>#199</a>).
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="install">Install</h2>
  <p>
    One command for Fedora, Ubuntu (including 26.10), Debian, Kali, Arch, CachyOS and openSUSE
    Tumbleweed on x86_64. Fedora, Ubuntu, Debian and Kali also have ARM 64-bit packages.
  </p>
  <pre tabindex="0"><code>{INSTALL_COMMAND}</code></pre>
  <p>
    The script picks the prebuilt package for your distribution from the latest GitHub release, asks
    before it downloads the package, checks the file against <code>SHA256SUMS</code> and installs it
    with your package manager. Your configuration is kept. Its prompts are in Vietnamese: answer <kbd>c</kbd> to continue. The source is
    <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/install.sh`}>install.sh</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    On GNOME, on KDE Plasma (Wayland), and on X11 desktops that pick the input method with im-config such
    as Xfce, Cinnamon and MATE, the script also makes fcitx5 the input method for your account and adds Ngó
    Sen to the input method list. Log out and back in, then press Ctrl+Space, fcitx5's default key, to
    switch Vietnamese on; the script does not change the key, and machines where an earlier version set
    Ctrl+Shift keep it. On GNOME it also hides the fcitx5 launchers you do not need, and
    adds the Input Method Panel extension when there is no system tray, as on Fedora. These steps apply to
    the account that runs the script; add <code>--no-setup</code> to install the package only. On KDE
    Plasma it selects fcitx5 as KDE's virtual keyboard (System Settings, Virtual Keyboard), so Qt apps
    such as Kate and Dolphin can type too.
  </p>
  <p>On other desktops, or after installing with <code>--no-setup</code> or a package by hand:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Restart fcitx5 with <code>fcitx5 -rd</code>. Do this again after every update. On KDE Wayland, log
      out and back in instead: fcitx5 started with <code>fcitx5 -rd</code> cannot type in Qt apps such
      as Kate and Dolphin.
    </li>
    <li>Open “Fcitx5 Configuration” and add the input method named Ngó Sen.</li>
    <li>On KDE Wayland, select “Fcitx 5” under System Settings, Virtual Keyboard.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    To update, run the same install command again, then restart fcitx5 with <code>fcitx5 -rd</code>;
    on KDE Wayland, log out and back in instead. For manual downloads, Ubuntu 26.10 uses
    <code>fcitx5-ngosen_*_stonking_amd64.deb</code>, or the same name ending in <code>_arm64.deb</code>
    on ARM 64-bit. Check the download against <code>SHA256SUMS</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Since version 1.5.3, each release is available through signed repositories for dnf (COPR), APT,
    pacman and zypper. See the <a href="/cai-dat/#repo-goi">repository commands</a> to add one.
    The shared key fingerprint is <code>6D4A 2075 F985 3BE8 F6B6 FE7E 7550 1858 158B 36CD</code>;
    compare it with what the package manager shows.
  </p>
  <p>
    Since 1.2 the settings window follows the desktop's look, light or dark, KDE or GNOME. Every change is
    saved at once, with an Undo button (Hoàn tác).
  </p>
  <picture>
    <source media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" srcset="/screenshots/settings-dark.png" />
    <img
      src="/screenshots/settings-light.png"
      width="750"
      height="500"
      loading="lazy"
      alt="The Ngó Sen settings window on CachyOS (GNOME) with the Breeze style, Gõ chữ page, with the Gõ Sen mode selected."
    />
  </picture>
  <p>
    On Ubuntu 26.04 the script turns on an extension that works around a GNOME bug that drops the
    backspaces the input method sends. After installing a package by hand, log out and back in, then run
    <code>gnome-extensions enable forward-keys@ngosen.github.io</code> once.
  </p>
  <p>
    To uninstall, run the script with <code>--uninstall</code>. On GNOME it also undoes what the script
    changed, so GNOME uses IBus again after you log back in. On Xfce, Cinnamon and MATE it resets the
    input method to the system default; fcitx5 stays installed and may still start, typing English only.
    On KDE it leaves fcitx5 as the virtual keyboard, since another input method may use it; choose None
    under System Settings, Virtual Keyboard to drop it.
  </p>
  <pre
    tabindex="0"><code>{`curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ngosen/ngosen/main/install.sh | bash -s -- --uninstall`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    To try unreleased changes, install the <a href={`${REPO}/releases/tag/next-build`}>test build</a>,
    built automatically whenever the <code>next</code> branch changes:
  </p>
  <pre
    tabindex="0"><code>{`curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ngosen/ngosen/next/install.sh | NGOSEN_RELEASE_URL=https://github.com/ngosen/ngosen/releases/download/next-build bash`}</code></pre>

  <h2 id="tested">Where it has been tested</h2>
  <p>
    Packages exist for these distributions on x86_64. Fedora 43 and 44, Ubuntu, Debian and Kali also
    have ARM 64-bit (aarch64) packages, and the script picks the one for your machine; <a href={ARM_TESTER.url}>{ARM_TESTER.name}</a> installed
    and ran the ARM package on Ubuntu 26.04 (a VMware virtual machine), and the other ARM packages are
    build-only. A package that builds is not a package that works,
    so each one is labelled by how far it was tested, as listed in the notes of release
    {RELEASE.version}.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Distribution</th>
          <th scope="col">Tested level</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          DISTRIBUTIONS.map((item) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{item.name}</th>
              <td>
                <span class="stamp" data-level={item.level}>
                  {levels[item.level]}
                </span>
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    “Main setup” means the maintainer types on that system every day, on KDE Plasma Wayland. “Tested”
    means a handful of apps were tried in a virtual machine: CachyOS with Hyprland, Ubuntu 26.04 with
    GNOME Wayland, Linux Mint 22 for Ubuntu 24.04 and MX 25 for Debian 13; the two Ubuntu tests used a
    build from source. “Builds only” means the package builds and passes the test suite at build time;
    nobody has typed with it there. These levels come from testing release 0.5; the spreadsheet fixes in
    1.0 were tested in a CachyOS virtual machine (KDE Wayland) with LibreOffice Calc, WPS Office and
    Google Sheets in Firefox. Release 1.1 renames the installed files; the update from 1.0.0-1 was
    tested in a Linux Mint 22 virtual machine (Cinnamon, X11): the settings were copied over, the input
    method stayed in the list and typed the right text. Release 1.2 replaces the settings window: saving
    and undo through a real fcitx5 were tested in Linux Mint 22 (Cinnamon, X11), CachyOS (KDE Wayland) and
    Ubuntu 26.04 (GNOME Wayland) virtual machines, and 1.2 typed the right text in GNOME Text Editor on
    CachyOS. The fix for swapped letters in gnome-terminal was tested with a simulated D-Bus client and
    by typing in gnome-terminal by hand: no letters were swapped. Release 1.2.1 only drops the Applications
    page (Ứng dụng) from the settings window; the input method is unchanged. The four-page window was
    opened in a CachyOS virtual machine (KDE Wayland), in light and dark. Release 1.2.2 fixes the label
    next to the cursor and lost tone marks in the Edge address bar; the Edge bug was reproduced in a test
    rebuilt from a key log on Fedora 44 (KDE Wayland), then typed again by hand in Edge after the fix.
    Release 1.3.0 adds the option for Backspace to remove only the tone mark, typed by hand on Fedora 44
    (KDE Wayland) with a build from the <code>next</code> branch. The new tray icons were checked in
    Linux Mint 22.3 (Cinnamon) and MX Linux 25.3 (Xfce) virtual machines. The Debian 13 package was
    installed in MX Linux 25.3, where Telex <code>vieejt</code> typed <code>việt</code> in a GTK terminal,
    and updating there from 1.0.0-1 and running <code>fcitx5 -rd</code> kept Ngó Sen in the input
    method list. Release 1.4.0 mostly changes the one-line installer. It was run in Ubuntu 26.04 (GNOME
    Wayland), CachyOS (GNOME 51), Linux Mint (Cinnamon, X11) and MX Linux 25 (Xfce, X11) virtual machines
    with a build from the <code>next</code> branch: after logging back in, Ctrl+Shift and then Telex
    <code>tieengs vieetj</code> typed <code>tiếng việt</code> in a GTK3 text field, 3 times out of 3 on
    each machine. Uninstalling was tested in Ubuntu 26.04 (IBus came back) and MX Linux 25. Release 1.4.1
    only changes the installer; the input method is the same as in 1.4.0. The KDE setup was run in a
    CachyOS virtual machine (Plasma 6.7 Wayland) on an account with no input method configured: after
    logging back in, Ctrl+Shift and then Telex <code>tieengs vieetj</code> typed <code>tiếng việt</code>
    3 times out of 3 in a Qt6 text field and 3 times out of 3 in a GTK3 one; before the fix the Qt6 field
    got raw keystrokes. With the 1.4.0 package, Chrome 155, VS Code, Discord, Firefox and GNOME Text Editor
    (Flatpak) typed correctly in an Ubuntu 26.04 (GNOME Wayland) virtual machine; Chrome forced through
    Xwayland was wrong 5 times out of 30. Release 1.4.2 is a bug-fix release, tested with a build from
    the <code>release/1.4.2</code> branch. In the Gemini prompt box in Chromium on a CachyOS virtual
    machine (Plasma Wayland), <code>ddown</code> at the start of an empty box typed <code>đơn</code> in 3
    of 3 sentences (before the fix it gave <code>n</code>). On Sway (CachyOS), a line starting
    <code>dduwowngf tieengs vieetj</code> typed <code>đường tiếng việt</code> correctly in a GTK3 text
    field and in Firefox. In an Ubuntu 26.04 (GNOME Wayland) virtual machine,
    <code>ddwowngf tieengs vieetj</code> typed <code>đường tiếng việt</code> 2 times out of 2 in Ptyxis
    and 2 times out of 2 in GNOME Console. Kali Rolling came in 1.4.2: the script was run in a Kali 2026.2
    (Xfce, X11) virtual machine. Release 1.4.3 is a bug-fix release, tested with an Ubuntu 24.04 package
    built from PR #111 in a Linux Mint 22.1 virtual machine (Cinnamon, X11, fcitx5 5.1.7): with
    <code>kạ</code> added to the custom dictionary, <code>kaj</code> typed <code>kạ</code> 2 times out of 2
    with the custom dictionary ticked and <code>kaj</code> 2 times out of 2 with it unticked. The same steps
    on 1.4.2 typed <code>kạ</code> both times. Release 1.5.0 was never published. The 1.5.0 packages were
    tried in Linux Mint 22.1 (Cinnamon, X11, <code>noble</code> package), Ubuntu 26.04 (GNOME Wayland,
    <code>resolute</code> package) and MX Linux 25 (Xfce, X11, <code>trixie</code> package, the first one
    with the Rust core) virtual machines: on all three, <code>tieengs vieetj</code> typed
    <code>tiếng việt</code> in a GTK3 text field, and on MX <code>nguwowif vieetj nam</code> typed
    <code>người việt nam</code>. On Ubuntu 26.04, choosing the Custom input method showed the hint in the
    settings window, and “Kiểm tra máy tôi…” (check my machine) found no setup problem. The 1.5.1
    packages were tried again on Mint and Ubuntu 26.04: with sentence capitalisation on,
    <code>ban. tooi</code>, Enter, <code>xin</code> typed <code>Xin</code> on the new line (1.5.0 typed
    <code>xin</code>), and plain typing still gave <code>tiếng việt</code>. Release 1.5.2 fixes the settings
    window and was tested in an Ubuntu 26.04 (GNOME Wayland, <code>resolute</code> package) virtual machine:
    on the Phím › Phím tắt page the key buttons of all five modes showed with the window small, maximized,
    and restored afterwards (1.5.1 lost them when maximized), and <code>tieengs vieetj</code> still typed
    <code>tiếng việt</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Version 1.5.3 (PR #151, after the final fixes) was tested by sending keys into virtual machines,
    using Gõ Sen and Telex. Each run typed the same line twice: <code>duyeet</code>, Space, 5 Backspaces,
    <code>yeejt</code> gave <code>duyệt</code>; <code>toan</code>, Space, 2 Backspaces, <code>nf</code>
    gave <code>toàn</code>; <code>ban</code>, Space, 1 Backspace, <code>f</code> gave <code>bàn</code>;
    then <code>tieengs vieetj xin chaof cacs banj, hoom nay trowif ddepj quas</code> gave
    <code>tiếng việt xin chào các bạn, hôm nay trời đẹp quá</code>. On Linux Mint 22.1 (Cinnamon, X11,
    <code>noble</code> package), a GTK3 text field, a GTK3 field forced through XIM and xed all typed
    correctly. xterm (XIM) was correct in 5/10 runs; the others had a few wrong words when keys arrived
    in a burst. Version 1.5.2 failed in 2/5 runs on the same machine, so this bug predates 1.5.3.
    On Ubuntu 26.04 (GNOME Wayland, <code>resolute</code> package), the GTK3 field, gnome-text-editor
    and xterm all typed correctly. Wine 11.20 Notepad passed all three Backspace cases, but 2/4 lines
    had one or two wrong words (<code>chaà</code>, <code>tiêễn</code>), compared with 4/4 wrong lines
    in 1.5.2 on the same machine. The remaining errors come from Wine.
  </p>
  <p>
    Builds from the <code>patch</code> branch after #143 and PR #149 (the same code as 1.5.3, with a
    different version number) were also tried with Gõ Sen and Telex in Ubuntu 26.04 (GNOME Wayland,
    <code>resolute</code> package), Linux Mint 22.1 (Cinnamon, X11, <code>noble</code> package) and
    CachyOS (KDE Plasma Wayland, Arch package) virtual machines. <code>duyeet</code>, Space, delete
    back to <code>du</code>, then <code>yeejt</code> gave <code>duyệt</code>; <code>toan</code>,
    delete one letter, then <code>nf</code> gave <code>toàn</code>; <code>ban</code>, Space, delete
    one letter, then <code>f</code> gave <code>bàn</code>. These were tried in gnome-text-editor, xed,
    GTK3 and GTK4 text fields, Ptyxis, gnome-terminal, Konsole, kitty, VS Code, Chrome and Firefox;
    holding Backspace and typing again also worked. The backspace-across-space case was tried with
    VNI too. LibreOffice and the snap build of Firefox still have that bug, but typing was not made
    worse.
  </p>
  <p>
    On 20 October 2026, a build from the <code>patch</code> branch, with the same program code as
    1.5.4-1, was copied over installed packages in virtual machines and tested with Gõ Sen and Telex.
    Typing <code>tieengs vieetj xin chaof cacs banj hoom nay trowif ddepj quas ddungs rooif</code>
    gave <code>tiếng việt xin chào các bạn hôm nay trời đẹp quá đúng rồi</code> on two test pages:
    an ordinary Draft.js field and a field redrawn after every key.
  </p>
  <p>
    On Ubuntu 26.04 (GNOME Wayland, <code>resolute</code> package), Chrome was correct in 6/6 runs
    with no lost commits, Firefox in 4/4 runs, and a GTK3 text field in 2/2 runs. With macros
    <code>e</code> → <code>em</code> and <code>ko</code> → <code>không</code>, typing <code>e ko a</code>
    gave <code>em không a</code> in the GTK3 field and Chrome. A real TikTok comment field in Chrome
    on Ubuntu had been tried while testing #176, with 23/23 words correct; it was not retested with
    the final package.
  </p>
  <p>
    On Linux Mint 22.1 (Cinnamon X11, <code>noble</code> package), Chromium was correct in 4/4 runs.
    On CachyOS (KDE Plasma Wayland, Arch package), Chromium was correct in 6/6 runs across the two
    Draft.js pages and an ordinary textarea.
  </p>
  <p>
    The 1.5.5 package from release PR #208 was tried in virtual machines on 20–21 October 2026,
    using Gõ Sen and Telex except for the VNI Windows run noted below.
    On Ubuntu 26.04 (GNOME Wayland, <code>resolute</code> package), in the address bar of Firefox
    installed from Flatpak during autofill, <code>gox</code> → <code>gõ</code>,
    <code>daaus</code> → <code>dấu</code>, <code>ee</code> → <code>ê</code>,
    <code>es</code> → <code>é</code> and <code>dduowcj</code> → <code>được</code> were each correct
    in 3/3 runs. With 1.5.4-3 in the same virtual machine, <code>ee</code> → <code>eê</code> in
    3/3 runs, <code>gox</code> stayed <code>gox</code> in 3/3 runs, and <code>daaus</code> →
    <code>daáu</code> in 3/3 runs.
    On Ubuntu 26.04 and Linux Mint 22.1 (Cinnamon X11, <code>noble</code> package), in GTK3 text
    fields with macros <code>VN</code> → <code>Việt Nam</code> and <code>q</code> → <code>“quá”</code>,
    typing <code>Vn VN q</code>, Space, Backspace, <code>s</code> gave
    <code>Việt Nam VIỆT NAM “quá”s</code> (1.5.4-3 gave <code>Vn VN “quá”s</code>).
    The 13-word sentence <code>tieengs vieetj xin chaof cacs banj hoom nay trowif ddepj quas ddungs rooif</code>
    gave <code>tiếng việt xin chào các bạn hôm nay trời đẹp quá đúng rồi</code> in both virtual machines.
    On Ubuntu 26.04, clicking inside the text immediately after typing in a GTK3 field was still
    recognised as a mouse click, correctly in 2/2 runs and one additional run with the final package.
    On Linux Mint 22.1 in xterm, reopening the last word still worked: <code>vieetj</code>, Space,
    Backspace, <code>s</code> → <code>viết</code>; with VNI Windows, <code>ban</code>, Space,
    Backspace, <code>f</code> → <code>baøn</code>.
    CachyOS, Fedora, Debian, openSUSE and Kali have not been tried with 1.5.5.
  </p>
  <p>
    NixOS uses the repository's flake instead of the one-line installer; the snippet is in the
    <a href={`${REPO}#cài`}>README</a>. CI builds it, runs the tests and loads the addon into fcitx5 on
    Xvfb, but nobody has tried it on a real NixOS machine yet, nor on Wayland, GNOME or aarch64.
  </p>

  <h2 id="known-issues">Known issues</h2>
  <p>
    Not fixed: SDL games not running on X11 ignore forwarded keys and get text without tone marks, and the
    snap build of VS Code types raw Telex; the workaround routes it through XIM, where about one sentence
    in ten gets a wrong tone mark. Telex is the method used and tested daily; VNI and the other methods
    work but have not been tested closely.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ngó Sen targets fcitx5 only. fcitx5 runs on GNOME, KDE and Xfce as well as Sway and Hyprland, has
    plenty of features and is light on RAM, and one input method framework is less to maintain than
    several. The old IBus code stays unmaintained on the <code>feat/ibus-engine</code> branch. Source, releases and the
    changelog are at
    <a href={REPO}>github.com/ngosen/ngosen</a>.
  </p>
</Page>

